diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5548e90
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,195 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any signed-in member can create bots from **Account settings → Set up chat bots**.
+The `/bots` page lists all of that member's active and suspended bots; administrators
+can manage all active and suspended bots. Bot names and optional avatars use the existing user presentation.
+
+Bots are users with the `bot` role and a creator. This deliberately reuses room
+membership, author attribution, avatars, mentions, message broadcasts, and push
+notifications. New bots do not join rooms by default. Select bots in the open or
+closed room membership editor. The **Add to all open rooms** checkbox on the new
+and edit bot forms defaults off. Enabling it joins all existing open rooms and
+future open rooms (including closed rooms converted to open). Disabling it keeps
+current memberships but stops automatic joins. Existing bots retain their current
+memberships when this feature is installed; automatic joining defaults off for
+them too. Bots cannot be promoted through account user administration.
+
+Deleting a bot deactivates it rather than removing its historical messages. The
+API rejects deactivated bot codes, so the old code immediately stops working.
+Deactivating a bot's creator does not deactivate the bot; administrators can still
+manage it.
+
+## Posting
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  https://chat.example.com/rooms/ROOM_ID/BOT_CODE/messages
+```
+
+The entire request body is UTF-8 plain text, including when curl uses its default
+form content type. JSON is also treated as literal text, not an envelope. HTML
+characters are escaped and line breaks preserved. The endpoint does not require
+a browser session or CSRF token and does not use a browser session to select the
+author. A bot must be a member of the target room.
+
+- `201 Created`, with `Location: /messages/:message_id` (the normal message permalink)
+- `422 Unprocessable Content` for blank or invalid UTF-8 bodies
+- `404 Not Found` for a missing room or one the bot cannot access
+- `401 Unauthorized` for an invalid, banned, or deactivated bot code
+- `429 Too Many Requests`, with `Retry-After`, during a posting cooldown
+- `403 Forbidden` for a suspended bot
+
+The text API does not currently accept attachments.
+
+## Credentials
+
+Codes are unique, random 32-character base58 credentials, not public identifiers.
+They are only displayed on the authorized, unpaginated management page. They are
+stored in the database because owners need to retrieve them; protect database
+backups accordingly. Bot pages disable HTTP and Turbo snapshot caching and send
+`Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`.
+
+The narrowly scoped `BotRequests` middleware prevents form/JSON parsing of the raw
+API body, keeping message text out of parameter logs. Codes are filtered from
+Rails parameters and request paths. **Configure reverse proxies, ingress/access
+logs, and external monitoring to redact the credential path segment as well.**
+Always use HTTPS, and avoid committing codes to repositories or printing them in
+CI logs. To revoke a leaked code, delete the bot and create a replacement.
+
+## Mention webhooks
+
+The new and edit bot forms have an optional **Webhook URL**. Clearing it disables
+callbacks. URLs must use HTTP or HTTPS; HTTPS is recommended because callbacks
+include message contents and the bot's posting credential.
+
+Active bots appear in mention autocomplete alongside people in their rooms.
+Creating a message with a rich-text @-mention queues one callback per mentioned
+bot that has a webhook and belongs to the room. Repeated mentions are deduplicated.
+Plain `@name` text and message edits do not trigger callbacks. Bot-authored messages
+do not trigger callbacks, preventing bot-to-bot reply loops.
+
+An Active Job worker sends an `application/json` POST after the message commits:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages" },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div class=\"trix-content\">… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Only the addressed bot's mention attachments are removed from `body.text`; other
+mentions remain, and `body.html` retains the complete rendered rich-text body.
+Both paths are relative to the Campfire origin. Use `room.reply_to` with the
+plain-text posting API above to reply later.
+
+A `200` response with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html` becomes a message
+attributed to the bot and broadcast normally. Plain text is escaped and preserves
+line breaks; HTML uses normal Action Text rendering and sanitization. `204` posts
+nothing. Other statuses/content types, blank or invalid UTF-8 bodies, and replies
+over 1 MiB are ignored.
+
+Network work runs entirely outside the message-posting request. Production needs
+the existing Resque worker running. Delivery rechecks bot status, webhook presence,
+and room membership. Deleted messages/bots are safely discarded. Requests have
+5-second connect and 10-second read/write timeouts, use the shared SSRF guard with
+a pinned public IP, and never follow redirects. Failed callbacks are not retried
+automatically to avoid duplicate commands. URLs and payloads are not logged by the
+delivery code; webhook form parameters are filtered from Rails logs.
+
+## Joined webhooks
+
+Adding an active bot with a webhook to an open or closed room queues a `joined`
+event after the membership commits, whether added manually or automatically:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+Direct conversations never send `joined`. Repeated grants, room edits, or enabling
+automatic joins for a bot already in a room do not send duplicate events.
+The callback represents a newly created membership, not an update to one.
+Existing memberships are not replayed when a webhook is configured later.
+
+Joined callbacks use the same transport, network restrictions, response limits,
+and text/HTML reply handling as mentions. A `200` text or HTML reply becomes the
+bot's message in the joined room; a `204` produces no message. Delivery runs in
+the background and rechecks status, webhook presence, and membership. Removing
+the membership before delivery discards that queued event. Failed callbacks are
+not automatically retried.
+
+Implementation note: membership grants retain bulk inserts for people, but bots
+use callback-enabled, idempotent creation backed by the unique room/user index.
+Keep future bot membership creation paths callback-enabled so they also send
+joined events. Do not perform HTTP requests from membership callbacks.
+
+## Bots per member
+
+Administrators configure **Bots per member** in account settings. The integer
+setting lives in the existing account JSON settings and defaults to 5, including
+for accounts saved before this setting existed. Zero disables new bots for
+members; administrators remain unlimited. Negative and blank limits are rejected.
+
+`User#can_create_bot?` counts the creator's active and suspended bots, matching the bot management
+page. Deleting a bot deactivates it and frees a slot. The creation-only validation
+in `User::Bot` also protects direct POSTs and model creation outside the management
+controller. Lowering the limit does not deactivate or prevent editing existing
+bots. Creation resumes once the member is below the limit again.
+
+The quota check runs inside Active Record's save transaction. The app's SQLite
+configuration uses immediate transactions, serializing writers across the count
+and insert. If the database adapter or transaction mode changes, preserve that
+serialization (for example, with a creator row lock) to prevent concurrent bot
+creation from exceeding the quota.
+
+Regression coverage is in `test/models/user/bot_test.rb`, the account and bot
+controller tests, and `test/system/bots_test.rb`.
+
+## Automatic safety stops
+
+The safety limits are fixed in `User::Bot::Safety`, not account settings:
+
+- Each bot can post 10 messages in a rolling 10-second window, across all rooms.
+  API messages and replies to both kinds of webhook share this allowance.
+- The next message is discarded and starts a 30-second cooldown. API requests
+  receive `429 Too Many Requests` with `Retry-After` rounded up to seconds left;
+  webhook replies are discarded. The triggering request is not a cooldown attempt.
+- Five additional posting attempts during that same cooldown suspend the bot.
+  Expiring a cooldown allows posting again and resets its attempt count.
+- Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries suspend the bot. Every non-2xx
+  response (including redirects), timeout, or connection error is a failure.
+  Any 2xx delivery resets the streak, even without a usable reply. A rejected
+  reply is not a delivery failure. Join and mention callbacks share the streak.
+
+Suspension returns `403 Forbidden` from the posting API and prevents webhook
+execution, including already-queued jobs. A request already in flight cannot be
+recalled, but its reply must pass the posting guard before becoming a message.
+Suspension does not delete messages or memberships, free an owner quota slot, or
+expire automatically. Owners and administrators still see the bot on the bots
+page, labeled **Suspended** with its reason. Only administrators see **Reactivate**;
+its POST action is administrator-only and the button uses native Turbo confirmation.
+Reactivation clears the reason, rolling window, cooldown, and delivery failure
+streak without changing the bot code, rooms, or messages.
+
+Safety state is persisted on the bot, rather than in a process-local cache. The
+rolling list has at most ten timestamps and is independent of message deletion.
+The posting guard locks/reloads the bot and commits message creation and accounting
+in one transaction. Rejection exceptions are raised *after* committing state so
+cooldowns and suspensions are not rolled back. SQLite's immediate transaction
+mode serializes writers; preserve that serialization if changing database setup.
+Webhook HTTP requests run outside database transactions; recording their outcomes
+and checking replies use short locked transactions.
+
+Regression coverage includes `test/models/user/bot_safety_test.rb`, the bot posting
+controller and webhook tests, and the bots page controller/system tests.
diff --git a/lib/middleware/bot_requests.rb b/lib/middleware/bot_requests.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..347e706
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/middleware/bot_requests.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Bot messages are raw text, even when curl sends a form content type. Keep the
+# body out of Rails parameter parsing (and parameter logging) altogether.
+class BotRequests
+  PATH = %r{\A/rooms/[^/]+/[^/]+/messages\z}
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if env["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST" && PATH.match?(env["PATH_INFO"])
+      env["action_dispatch.request.request_parameters"] = {}
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+
+  module FilteredPath
+    def filtered_path
+      super.sub(%r{(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages)(?=\?|\z)}, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+    end
+  end
+end
